+*   **Partial Volume Effect:** Interpolated density might be classified as a material not really present at the sample location, leading to false colors [S2].
+*   **Narrow Transfer Function Peaks:** If the ray sampling misses a specific narrow density peak in the transfer function, it may not be rendered, even with interpolation [S1, unofficial]. Pre-integrated transfer functions can mitigate this [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Staircasing Artifacts:** Avoiding false colors via prior segmentation can introduce severe staircasing artifacts due to high-frequency content [S2].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Pre-classification Shading Pipeline](7803-pre-classification-shading-pipeline.md)
-The Pre-classification Shading Pipeline (also known as Pre-DVRI) is a volume rendering method where voxel values are mapped to optical properties (color and opacity) and shaded *before* ray sample interpolation occurs. Unlike the Post-classification Shading Pipeline, which interpolates raw density values first, this approach classifies and shades the discrete grid samples, then composites them along the ray. It is distinct from post-classification primarily in its handling of partial voluming and its susceptibility to blurring at high image resolutions.
+The Pre-classification Shading Pipeline (also known as Pre-DVRI) is a volume rendering method where voxel values are mapped to optical properties (color and opacity) and shaded before ray sample interpolation occurs. Unlike the Post-classification Shading Pipeline, which interpolates raw density values first, this approach classifies and shades the discrete grid samples, then composites them along the ray. It is distinct from post-classification primarily in its handling of partial voluming and its susceptibility to blurring at high image resolutions.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of samples per ray and the number of rays (pixels) [G].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Linear with respect to the volume size for storage [G].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Scaling:** Performance scales with image resolution and volume size [G].
-*   **Strengths:** Avoids blurriness at high image resolutions (zoomed viewing or wide perspectives) because fine details in transfer functions are expressed in the final image [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Susceptible to false colors due to the partial volume effect, where interpolated densities are classified as materials not present at the sample location [S1]. Prior segmentation to avoid false colors can introduce severe staircasing artifacts [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When high-resolution detail preservation is critical and partial volume artifacts are acceptable or can be mitigated [G].
-*   **When not:** When partial volume effects cause unacceptable false colors and segmentation is not feasible or introduces too much noise [G].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of samples per ray and the number of rays. Each sample requires interpolation, transfer function lookup, and shading calculation.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the volume storage and transfer function size. Transfer functions are typically small lookup tables.
+*   **Performance:** Generates considerably sharper images than pre-classification methods, especially under magnification [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Resolves high-frequency detail in transfer functions; avoids blurring associated with pre-classification at high resolutions [S1, unofficial][S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Susceptible to false colors due to partial volume effects; may miss narrow peaks in transfer functions if sampling is insufficient [S1, unofficial][S2].
+*   **When to Use:** When sharp rendering of complex transfer functions is required and false colors from partial voluming are acceptable or can be managed.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When avoiding partial volume artifacts is critical and segmentation is not feasible, or when computational resources are extremely limited and simpler pre-classification is sufficient.
 
 ## Background
-The Post-classification Shading Pipeline, also termed Post-DVRI (post-shaded DVRI), was described in the context of comparing popular volume rendering algorithms [S1]. It is one of the two common optical models for volume rendering, depending on the order of classification/interpolation components [S1]. The algorithms differ mostly in the way interpolation and sampling is performed and also how interpolations are avoided in less relevant or irrelevant volume regions [S1].
+The Post-classification Shading Pipeline, also referred to as Post-DVRI (Post-Direct Volume Rendering Integration) or Post-shaded DVRI, is an alternative to the pre-classified model [S1, unofficial][S2]. It was developed to address the blurriness and inability to resolve high-frequency detail in transfer functions characteristic of pre-classification methods [S1, unofficial]. Early work in volume rendering often used pre-classification, but post-classification became more commonly used due to its ability to generate sharper images [S1, unofficial]. The method relies on the generalized volume rendering integral, requiring depth ordering for compositing [S1, unofficial].
 
